\section*{Systems with homogeneous components I}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
For each polynomial degree, a finite bound on the number of isolated periodic orbits of all real planar polynomial vector fields of that degree is claimed. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 143, 2026-09-24): Nested complex domains separate the required asymptotic scales, while high real jets retain a fixed coherence order through rapidly rotating dependent scales. Proper barriers and generic tilts reduce parameters in a derivative-stable analytic class; finite-dimensional counting then turns absolute finiteness into a uniform degree bound.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 143, 2026-09-24): The degree-uniform finite bound restricts to the specified polynomial families and is independent of their coefficients and cycle locations. It supplies a weaker finite upper bound, without the requested sharp two-cycle or (n+m)/2 count or the special simple uniqueness proof.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 143, the scope page documents the following selections. The selection concerns degree-at-most-five Liénard systems: two isolated cycles are the maximum and that maximum occurs. It is not the arbitrary-degree planar polynomial bound. Manuscript 1 is not among the papers selected by that page, so the family link does not formalize its main conclusion. It does not establish formal coverage of the complete problem clause.

This site has not executed the Lean files or checked their dependencies. These descriptions report the scope of the linked declarations, rather than a new verification of the proofs.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these results to an internal model. Its release notice cautions that results lacking full formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently assessed the proofs; a solved label records the scope of the reported claim.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item OpenAI, \emph{Uniform bounds for planar polynomial limit cycles}, 2026-09-24; citation key \texttt{OAI:uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026}. \href{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026.pdf}{PDF (GitHub)}; \href{https://github.com/openai/math/raw/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026.pdf}{PDF (direct)}; \href{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026/README.md}{Manuscript page and citation}; \href{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026/uniform-bounds-for-planar-polynomial-limit-cycles-September-24-2026.pdf}{Latest version}.
